                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "3aba2701-4b49-3c7e-bf8b-87c974c55d3e",
                        "entry_id": "8793e38d-997d-35b3-8699-221c2d6fbb45",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Soulier, grolle."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "colloquial",
                            "slang"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f1f39ce2-f62b-44a8-b872-83d2470161d4",
                                "sense_id": "3aba2701-4b49-3c7e-bf8b-87c974c55d3e",
                                "text": "O croquenots boueux, ô mes vieilles godasses,\nHéroïques sorlots, ô mes chers compagnons,\nJe vous aime, et pourtant, à travers vos crevasses,\nLe gel, cette nuit même, a mordu mes arpions !"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ad89bd24-d9d3-4a7d-baf6-70667cd21258",
                                "sense_id": "3aba2701-4b49-3c7e-bf8b-87c974c55d3e",
                                "text": "Il avait le cou noir, un liséré noir, laissé par son chapeau, sur le front, les doigts gris et les ongles noirs, une paire de gants de fil ternes à la main, des croquenots d’asile de nuit. L’entrée de cet olibrius provoquait un certain émoi."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0b11da59-ffbb-4d48-9ef3-8747eded7e1e",
                                "sense_id": "3aba2701-4b49-3c7e-bf8b-87c974c55d3e",
                                "text": "Il avait absolument besoin de ses « croquenots » pour onze heures."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0d4223bb-5848-4088-baa0-6cd897c8c44d",
                                "sense_id": "3aba2701-4b49-3c7e-bf8b-87c974c55d3e",
                                "text": "Lui-même chaussa en un tournemain ses vieux croquenots mal cirés, avachis et trop grands, puis enfila son pantalon qui lui pendait disgracieusement par derrière."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4836f7ea-a850-441f-9e7e-c4a31e071f30",
                                "sense_id": "3aba2701-4b49-3c7e-bf8b-87c974c55d3e",
                                "text": "Le temps était gris et mouillé\n La boue comme des œufs brouillés\n Sur ses cröquenots tout souillés…"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bcd0e34c-e9d3-4214-a1cc-99d5c070a626",
                                "sense_id": "3aba2701-4b49-3c7e-bf8b-87c974c55d3e",
                                "text": "Prenant peur, il freina brusquement des deux pieds, ses lourds croquenots ferrés faisant jaillir des gerbes d'étincelles."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "467f5ace-c442-4fba-8b77-7291d6863d6f",
                        "source_entry_id": "8793e38d-997d-35b3-8699-221c2d6fbb45",
                        "source_sense_id": "3aba2701-4b49-3c7e-bf8b-87c974c55d3e",
                        "target_lang_word": "clodhopper",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "clodhopper",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "ce8ecc54-fc3a-3cc3-a846-f0383414f954",
                                        "entry_id": "06f098e1-7a28-324f-b254-dbe80305a2f0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A strong shoe for heavy-duty use, a boot."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f748b2c5-4a18-47ca-a524-d8350f116316",
                                                "sense_id": "ce8ecc54-fc3a-3cc3-a846-f0383414f954",
                                                "text": "...who had got on his \"hill shoes,\" as he calls a pair of clodhoppers as thick as a ploughman's, and stuck round with nails."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1882778b-d637-3b8b-8500-f92b80b4fafb",
                                        "entry_id": "06f098e1-7a28-324f-b254-dbe80305a2f0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any shoe construed (within a particular context) as ungainly."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "US"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ad11c5c7-3ca6-48ff-81a4-45e93221d63a",
                                                "sense_id": "1882778b-d637-3b8b-8500-f92b80b4fafb",
                                                "text": "We had to walk slow because of his wooden clod-hoppers, and that was the way I wanted it now"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "63b760c2-3a1c-3e54-9142-8ac9ba029f7a",
                                        "entry_id": "06f098e1-7a28-324f-b254-dbe80305a2f0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "United States Navy ankle length work shoes, distinct from dress shoes or combat boots."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "slang"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "57889715-97a0-458b-984e-a2c2f2220640",
                                                "sense_id": "63b760c2-3a1c-3e54-9142-8ac9ba029f7a",
                                                "text": "Smiling Jim Mead of New York tries on his GI clodhopper boots. He decided to return them \"because we couldn't make any altitude with those aboard.\""
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4a822c1b-fda9-395c-a9fb-c34da9534ea2",
                                        "entry_id": "06f098e1-7a28-324f-b254-dbe80305a2f0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A peasant or yokel."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0def8cc9-53b5-4d1d-af8b-88173d783b1f",
                                                "sense_id": "4a822c1b-fda9-395c-a9fb-c34da9534ea2",
                                                "text": "[…] now a book is no greater rarity than bacon and greens in Virginia; and the clodhopper of this country returns from his daily labours to a book […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "79529075-3462-460a-982c-8ba214180b67",
                                                "sense_id": "4a822c1b-fda9-395c-a9fb-c34da9534ea2",
                                                "text": "'Nephew Jack,' he cried, looking at me when I was thinking what to say, and finding only emptiness, 'you are a heavy lout, sir; a bumpkin, a clodhopper; and I shall leave you nothing, unless it be my boots to grease.'"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "441448f6-df6e-37e5-a181-4d72821df29a",
                                        "entry_id": "06f098e1-7a28-324f-b254-dbe80305a2f0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A clumsy or foolish person."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "UK"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d666ca93-ea16-422e-b6f5-a7c26d9bd784",
                                                "sense_id": "441448f6-df6e-37e5-a181-4d72821df29a",
                                                "text": "All guess-work exploits shrivel up a good yard, or sometimes two, when brought to the measure, and the champion of the county dwindles into a clumsy clod-hopper."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a86dce97-5546-3a2a-be1e-5bf462c5dfe1",
                                        "entry_id": "06f098e1-7a28-324f-b254-dbe80305a2f0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Wheatear: any of various passerine birds."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0bb75575-88e9-447c-8f23-71e2832dcf7b",
                                                "sense_id": "a86dce97-5546-3a2a-be1e-5bf462c5dfe1",
                                                "text": "...and as the birds then begin to resort to the downs and open commons, the \"fallow-chat,\" \"wheat-ear,\" and \"clodhopper,\" are not unappropriate names."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
